body='''<!doctype html><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"><title>MinimalPairs: revenue model and approval plan</title><style>body{font:17px/1.6 system-ui;background:#faf8f3;color:#202520;max-width:1000px;margin:40px auto;padding:0 24px}h1,h2{font-family:Georgia}h1{font-size:40px}h2{margin-top:45px}table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:15px}td,th{padding:12px;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;text-align:left}img{max-width:100%}.note{background:#eaf0e8;padding:20px;border-radius:10px}code,pre{font-size:14px;white-space:pre-wrap}a{color:#335b79}</style>
<h1>What the data can predict, and what to do next</h1><p>Evidence cutoff: October 27 evening, 2026. Private owner analysis. Model and approval plan only; no new marketing or pricing launched by this document.</p>
<div class="note"><strong>Current evidence:</strong> six distinct paid accounts, $152 gross initial revenue, five lifetime and one monthly purchase. One monthly cancellation scheduled. This demonstrates some willingness to pay, not scalable demand, causal rescue uplift or subscription retention.</div>
<h2>The model in plain English</h2><p><strong>This is a sales-frequency forecast, not a fitted visitor-conversion model.</strong> We observed six buyers in about 24 days. Each simulation samples a plausible daily purchase rate, then a future buyer count, then how many buy lifetime versus monthly. It totals their initial payments. Repeating this 200,000 times gives the displayed uncertainty. The 2× and 4× scenarios manually multiply the purchase rate; they do not predict that our interventions will cause those gains.</p><p><a href="forecast.py">Read the exact forecast code</a> · <a href="forecast-results.json">Read all numerical outputs and assumptions</a> · <a href="purchase_hazard.py">Individual-model framework (not fitted)</a></p><h2>Visitors → buyers → revenue</h2><p>The recorded Plausible ratio is 3 tracked purchases / 759 reported visitors = 0.395%, approximately one tracked purchase per 253 visitors. Purchase tracking was incomplete. Six Stripe buyers cover a longer window starting October 4, whereas visitor coverage begins October 10; dividing six by 759 is not a valid reconciled conversion rate. These are reported visitors, not verified unique humans.</p><pre>Expected gross revenue = qualified visitors × purchase probability × average receipt
Visitors needed per buyer = 1 / purchase probability
Break-even acquisition cost per buyer = net contribution per buyer
Break-even cost per click ≈ purchase probability × net contribution per buyer</pre><p>Illustrative $29 lifetime offer: 0.5% conversion needs 200 visitors per buyer; 1% needs 100; 2% needs 50. To earn about $1,000 gross (35 buyers) requires 7,000, 3,500 or 1,750 visitors respectively. These are scenario calculations, not measured rates.</p><h2>Discount experiment proposal</h2><p>For comparable abandoned-checkout customers, randomly assign the same approved reminder timing to either the standard $29 offer or the $14.50 offer, keeping assignment consistent. Compare verified gross and net revenue per assigned eligible customer over 14 days, including later purchases and refunds. Half the price requires more than twice as many purchases to improve gross revenue. Low eligible volume means slow learning; no winner declared after one or two sales. This experiment is proposed, not activated.</p><h2>1. Model we can fit now</h2><p>Purchase arrivals follow a stationary Poisson process with uncertain rate λ. With six purchases in approximately 24 calendar days and Jeffreys prior p(λ) ∝ λ⁻¹ᐟ², the posterior is Gamma(shape=6.5, rate=24). Predict future purchases using Poisson(Hλ); mix $29 lifetime and $7 initial monthly receipts using q ∼ Beta(5.5,1.5). Renewals, costs, refunds and future discount uptake are not forecast. Counts are linked to verified Stripe payments, never inferred from checkout clicks.</p><pre>λ | data ∼ Gamma(6.5, 24)
N_H | λ ∼ Poisson(Hλ)
q | plans ∼ Beta(5.5, 1.5)
L_H | N_H,q ∼ Binomial(N_H,q)
R_H = 29 L_H + 7(N_H − L_H)</pre><p>The runnable forecast uses 200,000 seeded draws. Alternative 7-day and 14-day rate fits are in forecast-results.json. Strong stationarity assumptions and six observations make these conditional planning ranges, not calibrated guarantees. A simulated zero probability means no qualifying draw, not impossibility.</p><img src="forecast.png" alt="Revenue scenario medians and central eighty percent predictive intervals"><table><tr><th>Next 30 days</th><th>Median gross</th><th>80% predictive range</th></tr>'''+rows+'''</table>
<h2>2. Advanced individual purchase-time model</h2><p>A Bayesian hierarchical piecewise-exponential survival model describes purchase hazard while a visitor is at risk. Interval boundaries occur when observable behavior changes or a time bin ends. The likelihood uses exact time observed and treats departures or observation end as censoring, not permanent nonpurchase. Unknown cross-day visitors are never silently stitched together.</p><pre>ηᵢₖ = αₖ + b_state + b_language + b_source + b_device + βᵀxᵢₖ
λᵢₖ = exp(ηᵢₖ)
log L = Σᵢₖ [δᵢₖ ηᵢₖ − Δtᵢₖ exp(ηᵢₖ)]
b_group,j ∼ Normal(0, τ_group²); τ_group ∼ HalfNormal(0.5)
P(Tᵢ ≤ H | path, data) = Eposterior[1 − exp(−Σₖ Δtᵢₖ λᵢₖ)]</pre>
<p>Use time since entry, current funnel state, completed practice, diagnostic version/length, error counts and prior observed visits for identified returning accounts. Use target language and source with partial pooling. Freeze features at prediction time: no future checkout status, payment metadata or post-purchase practice as predictors. Unknown future behavior paths must be integrated over scenarios or an independently fitted transition model; holding a future path fixed is a conditional forecast.</p>
<p><strong>Status:</strong> purchase_hazard.py implements the likelihood, shrinkage priors, offline sampler and conditional purchase curve. Four mathematical unit checks pass. It is NOT fitted to production individuals. Aggregate dashboards do not supply historical person-time intervals; six purchases cannot support reliable segmented propensity. The sampler refuses fewer than 30 events, a conservative guard rather than proof that 30 is sufficient. Its basic R-hat is not enough: effective sample size, trace plots, temporal holdout and calibration remain required before production use.</p>
<p>Current anonymous daily journey retention is now 90 days. New accepted-event sequence and timing began October 28 UTC. Encrypted Plausible context remains 48 hours. Before the change, detailed anonymous journeys lasted roughly two days; lost records cannot be reconstructed. Retaining records does not create cross-day identity. Proposed account-linked cohorts require explicit known-user linkage within our backend, with no guessed identity.</p>
<h2>3. Optimize incremental revenue, not propensity alone</h2><pre>Choose action a to maximize:
E[Revenue within horizon under a − Revenue under control − incremental cost]
Subject to: functioning access, accurate claims, approved contact scope and tolerable refunds/errors.</pre><p>Someone likely to buy anyway is not necessarily someone who should receive a discount. At $14.50 versus $29, a half-price offer must more than double purchase probability to increase gross revenue, before considering fees or later purchases. Test incremental effects using randomized eligible holdouts, intention-to-treat denominators and fixed observation windows. Do not train on future outcomes or call a high propensity score causal lift.</p>
<h2>4. Exact approval package: 30 days, $0 new spend</h2><table><tr><th>Work</th><th>Execution</th><th>Acceptance / decision</th></tr>
<tr><td>A · Measurement, days 1–3</td><td>Keep the now-live retention fix. Add a private cohort export with stage times, release/offer version, censoring boundary and verified purchase linkage. Keep owner/test exclusions. Anonymous daily journeys and known accounts stay distinct. Fit aggregate baseline weekly; only shadow-score individual model after validation.</td><td>Replay one verified purchase and one unpaid path; no duplicate revenue. Explicit unmatched/unknown counts. No personal model deployed merely because code runs.</td></tr>
<tr><td>B · Conversion, days 3–10</td><td>Preserve free score + one example, $29 lifetime/$7 monthly. Make the paid next step directly match the learner’s tested contrast. One report-to-paid-practice CTA/placement test at a time, preserving a control. Fix errors and broken handoffs immediately.</td><td>Measure completed-diagnostic to plan, checkout and paid within 7 days; revenue per eligible report exposure. Daily operational review; no daily winner declarations.</td></tr>
<tr><td>C · Discovery, days 4–30</td><td>Prioritize six existing high-intent English/French entrances using current GSC queries and analytics. Improve answers, sound examples, titles and direct diagnostic paths. Seek ten relevant teacher/resource placements over the month, up to three permitted useful Reddit contributions weekly.</td><td>Track entrance-to-diagnostic and paid cohorts, earned placements and source attribution. Community rules apply; no invented testimonials or ranking promises. Exact new outbound email text/recipients shown before sending.</td></tr>
<tr><td>D · Lifecycle and quality, weekly</td><td>Operate already-approved course/founder/recovery messages with suppression and paid-user exclusions. Compare diagnostic-engaged leads against irrelevant bulk messaging. Prioritize high-use English/French clip reports and keep coverage checks.</td><td>Paid conversions, complaints/unsubscribes and audio failures. New wording requires approval; no broad extra discount or new spending.</td></tr></table>
<p><strong>Milestones:</strong> operational target 20 new buyers / about $500 gross over 30 days, not a baseline forecast. Check progress on days 7, 14, 21 and 30. If traffic stays flat and plan selection fails to improve, stop cosmetic iteration and focus on distribution plus customer evidence. If checkout failures appear, fix immediately. Maintain a simple weekly action ledger.</p>
<h2>5. Business assessment and owner contribution</h2><p>This presently resembles a focused one-time training product with organic discovery, not a proven recurring subscription business. Initial willingness to pay and real listening activity are encouraging. Six buyers cannot establish market size, acquisition scalability, strong retention or profitability after your costs. English/French focus is supported by usage; segment revenue rankings remain sparse.</p><p>$300–$500 monthly gross is a reasonable operating milestone to pursue, not a high-confidence guarantee. $1,000/month requires approximately 35 full-price lifetime sales or about 40 buyers at the current mixed order value. $3,000–$5,000 would require roughly 120–200 buyers/month at $25 per buyer and is an opportunity scenario, not an evidence-backed forecast. Add recurring value or teacher distribution only after concrete demand.</p><p>Owner time: two 30-minute English/French audio review sessions weekly, 15 minutes approving exact outreach/replies, and one 15-minute decision review. Optional asynchronous learner feedback is more useful now than adding languages or a pronunciation-scoring system. No face-to-face work required.</p>
<h2>Sources and reproducibility</h2><p>Inputs: private/rescue-overall-2026-10-27.json and verified Stripe aggregates from the same review. Source notes: <a href="https://www.pymc.io/projects/examples/en/latest/survival_analysis/survival_analysis.html">PyMC survival analysis</a>; <a href="https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/calibration.html">scikit-learn probability calibration</a>. These support methods, not business uplift claims.</p><pre>python3 rescue/company/revenue-model/forecast.py
python3 rescue/company/revenue-model/test_model.py
python3 rescue/company/revenue-model/build_report.py</pre>'''
